{"id":"96d4acac-0a32-41ac-95e7-edb9d8479937","object":"chat.completion","created":1785026048,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"from collections import defaultdict\\ndef solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    \\n    node_map = {}\\n    children_map = defaultdict(list)\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        node_map[node['id']] = node\\n        parent = node['parent']\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children_map[parent].append(node['id'])\\n    \\n    root_id = None\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = node['id']\\n            break\\n    \\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_set = set()\\n    removal_order = []\\n    \\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid in removed_set:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(\\n            child_id not in removed_set for child_id in children_map.get(nid, [])\\n        )\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        # tentative removal condition\\n        remaining_symbols = set()\\n        for n in nodes:\\n            if n['id'] != nid and n['id'] not in removed_set:\\n                remaining_symbols.add(n['symbol'])\\n        if required_symbols.issubset(remaining_symbols):\\n            removed_set.add(nid)\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n    \\n    retained_ids = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_set])\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removal_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement a function `solve(case)` that takes a payload with nodes and required_symbols, and returns an object with `removed_node_ids` and `retained_node_ids`. The task is \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" but the description mentions Perses syntax-guided reduction, Perses is a tool that implements a reduction algorithm based on a grammar. However, we are not given a grammar or parser. The input provides nodes with parent relationships, depth, symbol, removable flag, and utility. Also required_symbols. The objective is to remove removable nodes while preserving that all required symbols remain present somewhere in the tree, and we must never remove the root or a node with retained children. The algorithm: consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes; tentatively remove a node if all required symbols remain represented; use node ID as tie-breaker at equal depth. Also must ensure grammar validity? The description mentions \"grammar_valid\" as a hard contract. But without a grammar, what does that mean? Possibly we must ensure that after removal, the remaining tree still forms a valid parse tree according to some grammar? But we don't have grammar rules. Alternatively, maybe the grammar is implicitly defined by the parent structure and symbols? Possibly the condition is that after removing a node, its children become children of its parent? In Perses, reduction removes a node and replaces it with its children? Or deletes the node and its subtree? The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". It also says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" The fields are: nodes (with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility), required_symbols. Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That suggests that we cannot remove a node that has children that are retained (i.e., not removed). But the algorithm tries to remove nodes one by one. Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This is after removal, the set of symbols present in the retained nodes must cover required_symbols.\n\nGiven the complexity, we need to infer the intended algorithm from typical Perses reduction. Perses is a syntax-guided reduction algorithm that reduces a sequence of tokens to a smaller one while preserving a property (interestingness). It works on a parse tree, and tries to remove subtrees or replace them with smaller alternatives that are grammar-valid. The algorithm processes nodes in depth order (deeper first). For each node, it attempts to delete it (replace with its children) or replace it with a simpler alternative from the grammar. But we are not given alternative subtrees. The input has a \"utility\" field but that's likely not used. Also \"registered_candidate_order\" might refer to order of deletion candidates.\n\nMaybe the task is simpler: we need to \"reduce\" the tree by removing removable nodes subject to constraints. The phrase \"registered_candidate_order\" might mean we process nodes in a specific order: depth (descending) then by ID as tie-breaker. Then we try to remove each node if removable and not root and not a required symbol and not having retained children, and if after removal, all required symbols still exist somewhere in the tree (considering that removing a node also removes its descendants? Or does removal mean making the node's children become children of its parent? The phrase \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" suggests that we only remove leaf nodes? Or we can remove a node that has children only if those children are also removed? Actually \"retained children\" means children that are still in the tree after reductions. So if we remove a node, all its descendants are also removed (since the node is gone). So to avoid losing children that we want to keep, we must not remove a node if any of its children are still retained. So we can only remove a node if all its children are already removed. That matches a bottom-up reduction: we remove leaves first, then move up.\n\nAlso \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means after removing this node (and its subtree), the set of symbols in the remaining tree must still include all required symbols.\n\nThe algorithm:\n- Start with all nodes present.\n- Process nodes in order: sort by depth descending, then by ID ascending (stable tie-break).\n- For each node:\n   - If not removable: skip.\n   - If it is root: skip.\n   - If its symbol is in required_symbols: skip? But required_symbols are symbols that must be present. If a node has a required symbol, we cannot remove it because that would remove that symbol. So skip.\n   - If it has any children that are still retained (i.e., not removed): skip (because we cannot remove a node with retained children).\n   - Tentatively remove it: check if all required symbols are still present among the retained nodes (including this node's ancestors and other branches). If yes, then actually remove it.\n- Continue until no more nodes can be removed.\n\nThis is reminiscent of the \"delete\" operation in Perses where you delete a node (replace with nothing) only if the resulting tree still satisfies the property. But Perses also has \"replace\" options, but we only have delete.\n\nThe \"utility\" field might be used for ordering of candidates? But we are told to use depth and ID. Utility might be ignored.\n\nAlso \"registered_candidate_order\" might refer to the order we consider nodes: deeper first, then by ID.\n\nNow we need to implement this in Python. The function should return an object with `removed_node_ids` (ordered list of IDs that were removed, in the order they were removed) and `retained_node_ids` (sorted list of IDs that remain). We should not mutate input.\n\nLet's code:\n\n- Create a set of all node IDs.\n- Create a dict mapping id to node info.\n- Create a set of required symbols.\n- Create a list of nodes sorted by depth descending, then by ID ascending.\n- Create a set of removed IDs (initially empty).\n- For each node in order:\n   - If id already removed: continue.\n   - Check conditions:\n     - not removable: continue\n     - id is root? root has parent null. If id == root (from input, it's \"root\")? But input has node with id \"root\". So we can check if parent is None.\n     - if symbol in required_symbols: continue\n     - check if any child is still retained: we need to know children. Build a children adjacency from parent field. For each node, we can have children list. Then for current node, iterate over its children, if any child's id not in removed_set, then retain children -> skip.\n   - If passes: tentatively check required symbols. Compute the set of symbols of retained nodes (all nodes minus removed_set minus current node). If required_symbols is subset of that set, then we can remove. Add to removed_set and record removal order.\n- After loop, retained = all ids minus removed_set.\n- Sort retained alphabetically? The spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs\". So alphabetical? Or numeric? IDs are strings like \"root\", \"n1\", etc. So sort as strings.\n- Return dict with `removed_node_ids` (list in removal order) and `retained_node_ids` (sorted list).\n\nBut need to be careful: when we approve removal, we also need to ensure that the node has no retained children. That condition implicitly requires that all descendants of this node are already removed (since children are removed before parent). So the algorithm should work bottom-up. But the check is only immediate children. However, if children are removed, then the node may still have grandchildren that are not removed? Actually if children are removed, then the node's direct children are gone, so no immediate children retained. But grandchildren of a child that is removed would also be removed because the child subtree is gone. So if a child is removed, its descendants are also removed implicitly. So only immediate children matter. That matches.\n\nAlso root: we should not remove root even if removable? Input says root has removable false, but we can still skip.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Build nodes list from case['nodes'].\n- Build a dict: node_map = {node['id']: node}\n- Also build children mapping: children_map = defaultdict(list)\n  For each node, if parent is not None, add node id to children_map[parent].\n- Required symbols set.\n\nProcessing order: sort by depth descending, then id ascending.\n\nNow we need to simulate removals. Since we process in order and removal conditions depend on current state, we must update removed_set as we go. That's fine.\n\nOne nuance: what if a node's child is removed after we already considered the parent? Since we process deeper first, parent will be processed later. So when we process parent, its children that are removable would have already been removed if conditions met. So we need to check children retained status at time of evaluation. That's correct.\n\nEdge case: what if there are multiple nodes with same symbol? Required symbol must be present at least once. So after removal, we need that at least one node with that symbol remains.\n\nAlso the root might have symbol \"root\". Required_symbols includes \"root\". So root cannot be removed.\n\nNow we need to write the Python code. Ensure we don't mutate input.\n\nReturn JSON object.\n\nAlso note: The output schema says removed_node_ids is an array of strings, retained_node_ids is array of strings. We'll produce that.\n\nNow produce the response.\n\nBut the instruction says: \"Your entire response must be exactly one valid JSON object with the single key 'implementation'. The first non-whitespace character must be { and the last non-whitespace character must be }. Do not use Markdown code fences.\" So we output a JSON string with escaped Python code.\n\nI'll produce a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value as a string containing the Python source.\n\nMake sure to escape newlines and double quotes properly.\n\nNow write the Python code. I'll include necessary imports (from collections import defaultdict). Use standard library.\n\nLet me write the function:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    \n    # Build maps\n    node_map = {}\n    children_map = defaultdict(list)\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        node_map[nid] = node\n        parent = node['parent']\n        if parent is not None:\n            children_map[parent].append(nid)\n    \n    # Find root(s): node with parent None\n    root_id = None\n    for node in nodes:\n        if node['parent'] is None:\n            root_id = node['id']\n            break\n    \n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    \n    removed_set = set()\n    removal_order = []\n    \n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid in removed_set:\n            continue\n        # Check conditions\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Check if any child still retained\n        has_retained_child = False\n        for child_id in children_map.get(nid, []):\n            if child_id not in removed_set:\n                has_retained_child = True\n                break\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Tentatively remove: compute remaining symbols\n        retained_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] != nid and n['id'] not in removed_set]\n        # But we haven't added nid to removed_set yet, so we consider removal\n        # Check if required symbols still present\n        remaining_symbols = set()\n        for n in nodes:\n            if n['id'] != nid and n['id'] not in removed_set:\n                remaining_symbols.add(n['symbol'])\n        if required_symbols.issubset(remaining_symbols):\n            # Approve removal\n            removed_set.add(nid)\n            removal_order.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_set]\n    retained_ids.sort()\n    \n    return {\n        'removed_node_ids': removal_order,\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\n    }\n```\n\nBut we need to be careful: when computing retained_ids after loop, we include all nodes not in removed_set. That's fine.\n\nHowever, we used `retained_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] != nid and n['id'] not in removed_set]` inside the loop - that's a bit inefficient but fine.\n\nAlso note: The nodes list contains all nodes originally. When we compute remaining_symbols, we iterate over all nodes and exclude the current node and already removed ones. That is correct.\n\nBut we didn't consider that when we remove a node, its descendants are also removed, because we don't explicitly remove them. However, the condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" ensures that we only remove a node after all its children have been removed (so they are in removed_set). Therefore, the descendants are already removed individually (or via their parent). So we don't need to recursively remove children. Good.\n\nNow test with given fixtures. I'll mentally run through case 0.\n\nCase 0: nodes: root (depth0), n1(d1), n2(d1), n3(d1), n4(d2, parent n2), n5(d2, parent n3), n6(d2, parent n1). Required symbols: root, s1.\n\nRemovable: n2 True, n4 True, n6 True. Others false.\n\nSorted by depth descending: depth2: n4, n5, n6; depth1: n1, n2, n3; depth0: root. Then by id: n4, n5, n6 (ids alphanumeric: n4 < n5 < n6). Then n1, n2, n3; then root.\n\nNow process:\nn4: parent n2, removable True, symbol s1? Actually n4 symbol is s1? In case0: n4 symbol is s1 (from payload: \"symbol\":\"s1\" for n4). required_symbols includes s1, so skip because symbol in required? n4's symbol is s1, which is required. So cannot remove n4. So skip.\nn5: symbol s2, not required. parent n3. removable False -> skip.\nn6: symbol s0, not required. parent n1. removable True. Check children: none (no children of n6). Check retained children? None. Tentative: remove n6. Required symbols: root and s1. After removal, do we still have s1? Yes, n4 has s1. So remove n6. removal_order = ['n6'].\nn1: depth1, symbol s1, required -> skip.\nn2: depth1, symbol s2, not required. removable True. Check children: n4. n4 not removed (still there). So has retained child -> skip.\nn3: depth1, symbol s0, not required, removable False -> skip.\nroot: not removable -> skip.\n\nNow after first pass, no more changes? We processed all in one pass, but could there be later removals due to changes? Our algorithm iterates once over sorted list; after we remove a node, we continue to next nodes. But since we processed deeper first, and we removed n6, that might affect its parent n1? n1's child n6 is removed, but n1's other child? n1 has no other children? In this tree, n1 has child n6 only. So after n6 removed, n1 has no children. However, n1 is not removable (false). So no effect. n2's child n4 is still there, not removable. So no further removals. So final removed: ['n6']; retained: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. Sorted: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"].\n\nCheck if that matches expected? We don't have expected output but seems plausible.\n\nNow test case 1: similar but n4 is removable and its symbol is s1, required. So n4 cannot be removed. n6 symbol s0, removable. n2 removable but has child n4 retained. So same outcome: only n6 removed. Case2: n6 removable, symbol s0, removable; n4 removable symbol s1 required -> cannot remove; n2 has child n4 -> cannot. So only n6 removed in all three? That seems a pattern. But maybe there are cases where more nodes can be removed if they are not required and have no retained children. For example, if n4 were not required and n2's child n4 removable, then after